  "text": "It is now widely accepted among scholars that the traditional three‑term reasoning system, first outlined by the early thinker, seems to rely on the familiar rules of statements that are not broken down into parts. Yet that thinker treated this three‑term reasoning as the most fundamental logic, assuming nothing else beforehand. Since he was aware of many ideas that today form the basis of modern truth‑functional logic, one can argue either that he failed to see their broader relevance, or that he thought those ideas could be captured within his own system. While most commentators favor the first view, this paper briefly supports the second. The issue is not whether the early thinker proved the second claim, but whether any three‑term reasoning framework can do so. To answer affirmatively, we first point to several passages in the foundational treatise that already employ well‑known truth‑functional principles. We then discuss historical attempts to translate the logic of unanalysed statements into three‑term form, and finally show how a newly proposed system of such reasoning can recast those passages as ordinary three‑term arguments."
